  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStrong ridging over the eastern half of the CONUS will continue\nthrough most of the new week. A large surface high will stretch\nfrom the Tennessee Valley to New England for most of this time\nas well, with some shifts in orientation as another large sfc\nhigh pushes into the region from Canada. This will result in\ncontinued dry weather through most of this coming week. The\nexception to this will be the low chance for a couple\nshowers/sprinkles with a weak passing cold front later Monday. \nTemperatures will warm through Monday ahead of the cold front. \n\nTemperatures will cool for Tuesday behind the passing cold front\nwith day-to-day warming for Wednesday and Thursday ahead of the\nnext approaching cold front. A weak cold front will cross the \nregion later Thursday night into Friday morning, with a couple\nof showers possible with its passage. \n\nIt has been a week for most areas since any measurable rainfall\nhas occurred, and with another week of mainly dry conditions\nexpected, combined with summer vegetation dying with the fall \ntemperatures, the threat for fire weather concerns is\nincreasing. While strong winds aren't expected in the coming\nweek, some increased wind speeds with the weak passing cold\nfronts will be possible.",
